1076 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1006 0 R
/E (The Freedom in Africa Report \(F\(in\)AR\) is a flagship research initiative of the Liberalist Centre, an African policy and research organisation.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1077 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1006 0 R
/E (F\(in\)AR 2026 is the second edition of the report, building on the foundation of the inaugural F\(in\)AR 2025. The first edition established a new framework for examining freedom and prosperity across Africa, bringing together evidence on political rights, economic opportunities and the conditions that shape people's well-being. The 2026 edition continues this work while expanding the evidence base and providing a new assessment of how these conditions are changing across the continent.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1078 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1006 0 R
/E (The report was created in response to the need for a stronger African perspective on freedom. While international indices provide valuable information on democracy, governance, rights and economic conditions, they do not always capture the distinct political, institutional and social realities found across African countries. F\(in\)AR brings these areas together to provide a broader understanding of how freedom and prosperity are experienced across the continent.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1079 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1006 0 R
/E (F\(in\)AR examines three interconnected dimensions: Political Freedom, Economic Freedom and Prosperity. Looking at these areas together provides a better picture of the environment in which people exercise their rights, participate in society, pursue economic opportunities and improve their quality of life.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1080 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1006 0 R
/E (With each edition, F\(in\)AR aims to build a consistent record of change across Africa. The 2026 report therefore serves both as an assessment of current conditions and as part of a longer-term effort to track progress, identify setbacks and draw attention to areas requiring further reform.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1081 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1006 0 R
/E (Rather than treating country rankings as a competition or a final judgement, F\(in\)AR uses comparative evidence to identify patterns, differences and areas of progress. The findings can help reveal where institutions are becoming stronger, where opportunities are expanding and where significant challenges continue to affect people's freedom and prosperity.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1082 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1006 0 R
/E (F\(in\)AR collects and standardises publicly available information from reputable international sources before interpreting the findings within an African context. By bringing these different datasets together, the report seeks to make complex information easier to understand while providing a consistent basis for examining change from one edition to the next.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
