1089 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1006 0 R
/E (For governments and policymakers, F\(in\)AR can serve as a reference for identifying areas of strength and areas requiring attention. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
1090 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1006 0 R
/E (Changes in Political Freedom may highlight developments in elections, civic participation, judicial independence or the protection of civil rights. Economic Freedom indicators can reveal barriers affecting businesses, workers, investment and economic activity, while Prosperity indicators provide a broader view of the outcomes experienced by citizens.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
1091 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1006 0 R
/E (For researchers, journalists and civil society organisations, the report provides a structured body of evidence for examining developments within and across African countries. Its findings can support research, reporting, advocacy and public discussion.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
1092 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1006 0 R
/E (For citizens, F\(in\)AR offers a clearer way to understand the conditions shaping freedom and prosperity in their  countries and how these compare with developments elsewhere on the continent.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
1093 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1006 0 R
/E (F\(in\)AR is ultimately intended to support informed discussion and not to make a final verdict. Since the work began with the 2025 edition, the 2026 report contributes to an ongoing record of Africa's progress, challenges and changing freedom landscape.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
