1343 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1013 0 R
/E (Freedom of assembly is a fundamental part of civic life. It determines whether people can gather peacefully, organize around shared concerns, protest government decisions or demand change without facing excessive state interference.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1344 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1013 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1345 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1013 0 R
/E (Across Africa, however, the space to gather and demonstrate remains sharply divided. In some countries, restrictions on public demonstrations and political gatherings continue to make collective action difficult. In others, citizens appear to have gained considerably more room to organize and make their voices heard.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1346 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1013 0 R
/E (Nigeria recorded one of the most significant improvements, while Madagascar and Lesotho also moved strongly upward. The Gambia, Kenya, Liberia, Sierra Leone and Zambia experienced notable gains as well, pointing to a wider expansion of civic space in various sectors of the continent.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1347 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1013 0 R
/E (These developments suggest movement in a positive direction, but the ability to assemble still remains closely tied to the political environment in each country. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
