1545 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E (The 2026 results point to a more constrained mobility environment overall. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1546 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E (The disparity between countries shows that the freedom to travel, relocate and move freely within and across borders remains far from uniform in Africa.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1547 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E (Gambia declined from 9.5 to 8.7, Ghana from 9.1 to 7.2, and Benin from 9.1 to 7.3. The declines were broader in countries such as Nigeria, Namibia, Malawi and Mozambique. Eritrea, at just 0.1, recorded the lowest score.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1548 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E (There were exceptions to the downward trend. Seychelles improved from 8.7 to 9.6, while Mauritius also gained, reaching 7.4. These countries contrast with states where conflict, security concerns or restrictive government policies can significantly limit mobility.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1549 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E (Overall, freedom of movement weakened noticeably in 2026, with most countries recording lower scores than in 2025. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
1550 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E (2.3 Free Trade)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
1551 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E (Free Trade weakened across much of the 2026 dataset. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
1552 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
1553 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E (It argues that reducing these domestic frictions is central to making the African Continental Free Trade Area \(AfCFTA\) work in practice. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
1554 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E (This challenge is particularly important because intra-African trade remains relatively limited. The United Nations Economic Commission for Africa \(UNECA\) says AfCFTA could help reduce commodity dependence, expand manufacturing and strengthen regional value chains, but only if countries implement the necessary reforms. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
1555 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E (Against this backdrop, the 2026 scores suggest that Africa's trade environment remains uneven, with a few relatively open economies contrasting sharply with countries where border, regulatory and market restrictions continue to constrain commerce.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
1556 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E (Mauritius remained the standout performer, while Kenya, Equatorial Guinea, and Chad,  registered sizeable setbacks.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
