1799 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1024 0 R
/E (Education remains one of the strongest foundations of long-term prosperity, yet access to it across Africa continues to be shaped by insecurity, poverty and the limited capacity of national education systems. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1800 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1024 0 R
/E (The United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization \(UNESCO\) reports that progress in keeping children in school has slowed sharply in sub-Saharan Africa, with population growth and conflict being amongst the factors disrupting improvements.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1801 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1024 0 R
/E (UNESCO also reports documented attacks on schools and education across West and Central Africa, including Burkina Faso, Cameroon, the Democratic Republic of Congo, Mali, Niger, and Nigeria. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1802 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1024 0 R
/E (While there are improvements in certain regions, the education system in others is still plagued with persistent insecurity, weak infrastructure and limited resources placing renewed pressure on access to education.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1803 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1024 0 R
/E (In these regions schools can become inaccessible, unsafe and years of educational progress can quickly be lost. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1804 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1024 0 R
/E (What emerges then in Africa is not simply a question of how many children are in school, but whether African education systems can provide stable, safe and meaningful access to learning as populations continue to grow.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1805 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1024 0 R
