1853 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1025 0 R
/E (3.5 Economic Inequality)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
1854 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1025 0 R
/E (Economic inequality is more than the gap between the rich and the poor. It also asks who has access to jobs, education, healthcare, land and other opportunities that allow people to improve their lives and build greater financial security.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
1855 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1025 0 R
/E (A growing economy can still leave large parts of the population behind when these opportunities remain concentrated among a smaller share of society. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
1856 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1025 0 R
/E (The World Bank continues to identify inequality as a major obstacle to inclusive growth in Africa, noting that unequal access to jobs, education, healthcare and productive assets can prevent economic growth from translating into broader improvements in living standards.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
1857 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1025 0 R
/E (Where these opportunities are limited, poverty can persist.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
1858 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1025 0 R
/E (Economic growth may increase national wealth but may not necessarily translate into better living conditions for everyone, because of high inequality)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
1859 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1025 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
1860 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1025 0 R
/E (3.6 Peace & Stability)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
1861 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1025 0 R
/E (Peace and stability are fundamental to prosperity: where violence and political instability persist, businesses struggle to operate, communities are displaced, and governments find it harder to provide essential services. A stable environment, by contrast, gives households and investors greater confidence to plan for the future.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [25]
>>
endobj
1862 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1025 0 R
/E (In the 2026 figures, Mauritius maintained a its score from last year, while Botswana and Ghana improved. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [26]
>>
endobj
1863 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1025 0 R
/E (Some of the largest improvements came in countries previously facing severe instability: Sudan rose from 1.1 to 6.2, while Burkina Faso and Ethiopia both moved from 1.9 to 4.3.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
1864 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1025 0 R
/E (Yet serious instability remains concentrated in several states. South Sudan, Tanzania and Somalia remain countries of particular concerns.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
