1892 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1026 0 R
/E (3.7 Employment & Entrepreneurship)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1893 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1026 0 R
/E (Figure 24. Employment Scores among African countries, 2026)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1894 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1026 0 R
/E (Employment and entrepreneurship are closely linked across Africa, but the relationship is not always straightforward. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1895 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1026 0 R
/E (A strong labor market should provide people with meaningful choices: they should be able to find formal employment, build a business or work independently based on opportunity rather than necessity. Where formal jobs are limited however, self-employment often becomes the main alternative.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1896 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1026 0 R
/E (The 2026 figures highlight just how different these realities are. Some countries show stronger conditions for employment and entrepreneurship, while others combine weak employment outcomes with very high levels of self-employment. Nigeria, for example, has a particularly high share of people working for themselves.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1897 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1026 0 R
/E (Self-employment accounts for 96% of workers in Chad, 93% in Sierra Leone, 92% in Madagascar and 89% in Nigeria. Burkina Faso, Burundi, the Central African Republic and Ethiopia also recorded very high levels. By comparison, the share is only 1% in South Africa and 2% in Mauritius.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1898 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1026 0 R
/E (The International Labor Organization \(ILO\) has repeatedly pointed to the dominance of informal employment across African labor markets. For millions of workers, earning a living outside the formal economy means operating without stable contracts, reliable social protection or easy access to finance.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1899 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1026 0 R
/E (This creates an important distinction between entrepreneurship as opportunity and entrepreneurship as necessity. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
1900 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1026 0 R
/E (A high level of self-employment can demonstrate initiative and resilience, but it can also reveal how difficult it is for workers to secure formal jobs. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
