2001 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1029 0 R
/E (Mauritius records the highest overall freedom score in the 2026 dataset, at 84.2, followed very closely by Seychelles, then Ghana, Cape Verde, Botswana and South Africa. These countries stand out because they perform relatively well across the three main areas measured in the report: political freedom, economic freedom and prosperity.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2002 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1029 0 R
/E (Southern Africa shows the strongest overall performance, with South Africa, Botswana, and Namibia all among the continent's highest-ranked countries. West Africa also performs strongly, led by Ghana, Cape Verde, The Gambia and Senegal. )
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
2003 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1029 0 R
/E (North and Central Africa, however, contain a large concentration of low-scoring countries. East Africa presents the widest contrast, with high-performing countries such as Seychelles alongside much weaker performers including South Sudan and Eritrea.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
2004 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1029 0 R
/E (The differences between regions show that freedom is not determined by one issue alone; strong political institutions, economic opportunity, access to public services, security and respect for individual rights all contribute to how countries perform.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
2005 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1029 0 R
/E (Southern Africa records the strongest regional performance in the dataset. However, the region is not uniformly strong. Zambia and Zimbabwe score considerably low compared to the rest, showing that countries in the same region can have very different political and economic conditions.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
2006 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1029 0 R
/E (South Africa stands out at the top with an overall score of 70.3. Its performance is relatively unbalanced across the three pillars, scoring 86.5 in political freedom, 63.0 in economic freedom and 61.7 in prosperity. )
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
2007 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1029 0 R
/E (Freedom House classifies South Africa as Free, while also pointing to challenges including corruption and weaknesses in due process. )
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
2008 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1029 0 R
/E (Botswana provides another example of institutional strength. The country's 2024 general election resulted in the first peaceful transfer of power from one governing party to another since independence. The change of government demonstrated that political competition and electoral transitions can take place without destabilizing the political system.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
2009 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1029 0 R
/E (Still, the region's strong freedom scores do not mean that its economic challenges have disappeared. The African Development Bank has highlighted continued concerns around infrastructure, economic diversification, and financing.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
2010 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1029 0 R
/E (Overall, Southern Africa's strength comes from a combination of relatively stable institutions, stronger economic systems and higher levels of prosperity. But the differences within the region remain significant, with some countries still struggling to achieve broader gains in freedom and living standards.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
