2112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1030 0 R
/E (The story of West Africa is not simply one of political decline. Some of its economies continue to show resilience, with growth supported by agriculture, investment and infrastructure. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1030 0 R
/E (On that side are regions where economic institutions remain relatively strong. Their stronger performance is a reflection of more established democratic systems and greater room for political participation. Ghana offers one of the clearest examples of what this difference looks like on the ground. Its political system has remained competitive, with regular peaceful elections, change in government, and a strong tradition of public participation. That political stability has also been matched by relatively strong economic conditions. While Ghana still faces challenges such as inequality, unemployment and pressure on public services, its institutions give citizens and businesses more room to organize, speak out and pursue opportunities than in many neighboring countries.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1030 0 R
/E (As we begin to move further down the regions into the Sahel however, the picture changes. With numerous coups, insecurity and military rule, putting pressure on democratic freedom in these states, the freedom scores of these countries are low. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1030 0 R
/E (Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger have experienced coups and growing insecurity, while military governments have tightened their hold on power.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1030 0 R
/E (Freedom House's 2026 assessment points to the continued consolidation of military rule in these countries and a wider decline in political freedoms.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1030 0 R
/E (East Africa contains some of the widest differences in freedom across the continent. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
2118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1030 0 R
/E (Seychelles stands at the top of the region, with strong performance across all three areas measured by the report. Mauritius follows closely.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
2119 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1030 0 R
/E (Kenya also has a relatively strong political environment, with space for political participation and public debate, but its economic freedom and prosperity scores are much more on the moderate side. Tanzania and Uganda sit further down the regional ranking, while Rwanda presents a very different model altogether and is particularly revealing; Its prosperity score is considerably stronger than its political freedom score, showing that improvements in economic and social conditions do not always come with greater political freedom. The country has made progress in areas such as development and public services, while political opposition and civic space remain more restricted.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
2120 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1030 0 R
/E (As we move further down however, the picture becomes much more difficult. South Sudan, Eritrea and Somalia remain among the region's weakest performers. Conflict, insecurity, political restrictions and fragile institutions continue to limit both individual freedom and economic opportunity. South Sudan is especially affected, with very low scores across political freedom, economic freedom and prosperity.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
