2276 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1032 0 R
/E (Southern Africa has a strong foundation to build on. The next step is to turn institutional stability into broader economic opportunity and better living conditions. Countries such as South Africa, Botswana, Namibia and Mauritius can focus on reducing unemployment, improving infrastructure, supporting private enterprise and making growth more inclusive. For countries performing less strongly, the priority is to strengthen institutions and create a more predictable environment for investment and jobs. The region shows that strong political systems can provide a foundation for prosperity, but that foundation still needs to reach more people.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2277 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1032 0 R
/E (West Africa needs to protect the democratic gains already made while addressing the insecurity and economic pressures affecting parts of the region. Ghana, Cabo Verde and Senegal show what relatively stable institutions can produce, while the experience of the Sahel shows how quickly political instability can reverse progress. A way forward will require stronger democratic institutions, credible elections, accountable governments and greater investment in jobs and basic services. Tackling insecurity is equally important, because lasting economic development becomes difficult when citizens and businesses cannot operate safely.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1032 0 R
/E (East Africa faces a different challenge: closing an unusually wide gap between countries. The region has examples of strong political freedom and prosperity alongside countries facing conflict, repression and extreme institutional weakness. The priority should be to strengthen basic institutions, expand civic freedoms and improve access to education, healthcare, infrastructure and economic opportunity. The next challenge is ensuring that development is accompanied by greater space for citizens to participate and hold power accountable.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1032 0 R
/E (North Africa needs to narrow the distance between economic development and political freedom. Several countries have built stronger economic and social systems without achieving the same level of political openness. The way forward is therefore not simply faster growth, but greater accountability, stronger civic institutions, wider political participation and more room for independent media and civil society. At the same time, countries affected by conflict and instability will need to restore basic security and functioning institutions before wider economic gains can take hold.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2280 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1032 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
2281 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1032 0 R
/E (Africa does not need one single model of progress. Each region has different obstacles, and its priorities must reflect them. The countries that make the greatest gains will be those that treat political rights, economic opportunity, and prosperity as connected parts of the same development process.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
2282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1032 0 R
/E (The Way Forward: Five Regional Priorities)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
