2294 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (Ease of travel helps economies grow. It boosts tourism, business, and trade between countries. A World Bank report shows that improving travel services could increase trade across Africa by up to 64% by 2035. Today, strict visa rules still stop many businesses and workers from growing.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2295 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (The travel rules are changing, but progress remains slow. Recently, the number of ports across Africa offering visa-free entry increased to 814. However, ports that still require travellers to obtain a regular visa also increased to 1,463.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (Still, things are moving forward. Togo recently opened its borders to all African travelers for visa-free entry. This makes it the fifth country to do so, joining Kenya, The Gambia, Benin, and Rwanda. Soon, Central Africa will open up too, as Chad and the Republic of the Congo plan to drop all visa rules for Africans on January 1, 2027.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (Right now, traveling smoothly across the whole continent is not yet normal. The next big step is to connect these separate country rules so that all Africans can travel, trade, and study anywhere with fewer problems.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (Regional differences across the continent remain significant. Travel rules in Central Africa are considerably more restrictive than those in East Africa, highlighting a major gap in mobility between the two regions. )
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (However, meaningful progress is underway even without a unified, continent-wide system. Today, 33 African nations allow visa-free entry to citizens from at least ten other countries on the continent, courtesy of reginal economic blocs.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
2300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (ECOWAS has the strongest regional free-travel system in Africa. Its members allow 100% visa-free travel, meaning citizens can visit other ECOWAS countries without any visa paperwork. This system started with a special agreement in 1979 and uses a shared ECOWAS passport.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
2301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (However, the group faced a major shift when Burkina Faso, Mali, and Niger left the alliance, reducing the bloc to 12 countries. Even with this change, visa-free travel remains fully active among the remaining 12 nations. )
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
2302 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (To make regional travel and business even easier, ECOWAS leaders are moving ahead with plans to launch a single regional currency, called the ECO, by 2027. )
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
2303 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (While passport-free travel is still a core rule, security checks have become stricter along the borders shared with the countries that left the group. )
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
2304 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (EAC is one of the easiest regions to travel through in Africa. It ranks second out of the eight regional groups for open borders. Within this group, more than half of the countries allow each other's citizens to enter completely visa-free.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
2305 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (Rwanda and Kenya are the most welcoming countries in the region. In fact, Kenya recently removed its online travel authorization \(eTA\) fees for most African citizens to make travel even faster. Somalia also signed a new deal to allow easier entry for Tanzanian citizens. However, countries like South Sudan, and Somalia still have strict travel rules for most other visitors. For example, Somalia requires most travelers to apply for an online e-Visa before they fly.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
2306 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (COMESA is one of the biggest regional groups on the continent, with 21 member countries. It ranks fourth out of the eight regional groups for open borders. )
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
2307 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (Kenya and Eritrea made the biggest travel improvements in the region. Kenya became much more welcoming by removing its online travel authorization \(eTA\) requirements for most African citizens. )
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
2308 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (However, traveling within COMESA can still be difficult. Only 17% of countries in this group share mutual visa-free travel rules, which is the lowest rate of any regional group on the continent. In addition, nearly half of the travel routes in COMESA have conflicting rules depending entirely on which country a traveler is coming from.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
2309 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (To fix these travel barriers, COMESA leaders met to fast-track changes. They are working to get more member states to fully adopt a 90-day visa-free rule to make trading and visiting each other much easier.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
2310 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (25% of African countries offer visas on arrival.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
2311 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (28% offer visa-free travel to African travelers.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
2312 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1034 0 R
/E (47% still require travelers to get a visa before departure.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
2313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1034 0 R
/T (This chart highlights the breakdown of entry rules across all cross-border travel routes on the continent. The data shows a positive shift toward easier access, with a combined 53% of travel paths no longer requiring a strict prior visa. This progress is driven by a steady climb in Visas on Arrival \(25%\) and Visa-Free travel \(28%\), which has successfully pushed pre-departure visa restrictions down to 47% continent-wide.)
/E (This chart highlights the breakdown of entry rules across all cross-border travel routes on the continent. The data shows a positive shift toward easier access, with a combined 53% of travel paths no longer requiring a strict prior visa. This progress is driven by a steady climb in Visas on Arrival \(25%\) and Visa-Free travel \(28%\), which has successfully pushed pre-departure visa restrictions down to 47% continent-wide.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
