/P 1035 0 R
/E (The Arab Maghreb Union \(AMU\) includes Algeria, Libya, Mauritania, Morocco, and Tunisia. It is the hardest regional group to travel through in Africa. Its travel openness score has dropped to its lowest point since tracking began in 2016. )
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
2444 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1035 0 R
/T (Moving between these neighboring countries is highly restricted. Previously, 70% of travel routes within the group were visa-free, but that has now dropped to 60%. While Algeria and Tunisia are still fairly welcoming to other AMU citizens, new rules in Algeria, Morocco, and Mauritania have made traveling much tougher. Libya remains the most strict, requiring a traditional visa from almost every African country.)
/E (Moving between these neighboring countries is highly restricted. Previously, 70% of travel routes within the group were visa-free, but that has now dropped to 60%. While Algeria and Tunisia are still fairly welcoming to other AMU citizens, new rules in Algeria, Morocco, and Mauritania have made traveling much tougher. Libya remains the most strict, requiring a traditional visa from almost every African country.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
2445 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1035 0 R
/T (Traveling through this region has become even more complicated because Morocco started requiring a mandatory online travel authorization \(ETA\) for visitors from neighboring countries. Because the AMU is struggling to work together, Algeria, Tunisia, and Libya have started meeting on their own to create a separate group to try and make border security and travel a bit easier between the three nations.)
/E (Traveling through this region has become even more complicated because Morocco started requiring a mandatory online travel authorization \(ETA\) for visitors from neighboring countries. Because the AMU is struggling to work together, Algeria, Tunisia, and Libya have started meeting on their own to create a separate group to try and make border security and travel a bit easier between the three nations.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [19]
>>
