<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1036 0 R
/E (The clearest sign of shrinking digital freedom is the rise in internet shutdowns. Teams at Access Now recorded 30 shutdowns across 15 African countries, which is up from 21 shutdowns previously. Most blackouts happened during political protests, instability, or elections. Tanzania had the most disruptions, cutting the internet eight times.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2475 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1036 0 R
/E (Governments are also blocking apps, making strict cybercrime laws, and tracking people online. According to Freedom House, South Africa is the only country ranked Free with a score of 73/100. Ghana \(64\), Nigeria \(59\), and Uganda \(52\) are ranked Partly Free. Rwanda remains highly restricted, while Ethiopia is ranked Not Free with a low score of 30.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2476 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1036 0 R
/E (These internet blackouts hurt the economy by blocking mobile payments, online shopping, and remote work. The World Bank warns that a nationwide shutdown in Kenya can cost $1.2 million in GDP for every two hours the internet is down.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2477 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1036 0 R
/E (To help more people get online safely and cheaply, the GSMA launched a new partnership to build and sell special $40 4G smartphones in countries like Nigeria, Uganda, and Rwanda. Keeping the internet reliable, open, and cheap is now critical for Africa's future growth.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
2478 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1036 0 R
/T (Africa is becoming increasingly dependent on digital connectivity for economic growth and civic participation, as such, protecting reliable and open internet access is increasingly important.)
