2490 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1037 0 R
/E (Across Africa, young people and civil society groups are becoming powerful forces for political change. This is especially true where governments are weak or public trust is low. The massive size of this challenge is clear from the CIVICUS Monitor. Their data shows that 44 out of 50 countries in Sub-Saharan Africa have restricted, repressed, or fully closed civic spaces. Today, more than 80% of the population in this region lives under these strict rules. )
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2491 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1037 0 R
/E (Despite these barriers, youth-led groups continue to fight corruption, high living costs, police abuse, and bad governance. Kenya is one of the clearest examples. Huge, youth-led protests in 2024 forced the government to drop a controversial tax bill, but the state's response was brutal, causing 60 deaths and 1,200 arrests. When rallies returned, over 65 people were killed and 1,500 were detained. In major 2026 updates, a report by Amnesty International Kenya showed that protests spiked by 29% in early 2026, leading to over 1,000 fresh arrests and documented cases of police torture during new economic rallies. )
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2492 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1037 0 R
/E (Uganda shows a similar pattern of youth activism facing heavy pressure. Young people have marched against government corruption, political abuse, and local environmental dangers. However, authorities have responded by blocking demonstrations and locking up leaders. Human Rights Watch documented multiple arrests of young activists who spoke out against the major East African Crude Oil Pipeline.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2493 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1037 0 R
/E (The overall data clearly shows this growing tension. Police locked up protestors in at least 19 African countries, and security forces used excessive, violent force in at least 20 nations. At the same time, youth movements are expanding past traditional politics. They are using social media for digital activism, starting environmental campaigns, fighting corruption, and organizing local communities.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2494 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1037 0 R
/E (Young people are increasingly willing to challenge power and demand honest leaders. However, turning these street protests into lasting, legal government reform remains heavily limited by police crackdowns, weak courts, and restricted political space.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
