endobj
2548 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (F\(in\)AR therefore does not point to a single policy solution for the continent. Different countries and regions require different priorities. However, the evidence identifies several areas where governments, regional organisations, and continental institutions can take practical action.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2549 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1039 0 R
/T (Strengthen institutions before crises expose them)
/E (Strengthen institutions before crises expose them)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2550 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (The first priority should be to build institutions that can function independent of individual political leaders. F\(in\)AR shows that countries with stronger political institutions generally have greater room for participation, more predictable economic environments, and stronger overall outcomes. At the same time, parts of the continent continue to experience political instability, military rule, weak checks on executive power and declining public trust.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2551 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (Governments should therefore strengthen constitutional safeguards, independent oversight bodies, legislatures and courts. Judicial appointments should be transparent and based on professional merit, while courts should have sufficient security of tenure and institutional independence to make decisions without political pressure.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2552 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (This is consistent with recent work by the International Institute for Democracy and Electoral Assistance \(International IDEA\), which recommends stronger institutions capable of resisting political manipulation, greater judicial independence and stronger protections for civic space.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
2553 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (African governments should also strengthen accountability outside election periods. Parliamentary oversight, public procurement systems, anti-corruption institutions and access to government information should be treated as permanent parts of democratic governance rather than mechanisms activated only during political crises.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
2554 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1039 0 R
/T (Turn Africa's youth population into productive employment)
/E (Turn Africa's youth population into productive employment)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
2555 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
2556 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (Policy should therefore focus on productivity and the quality of work, not just the number of jobs announced. Governments should reduce the cost of starting and operating businesses, expand access to finance, improve electricity and transport infrastructure, strengthen digital connectivity and make it easier for small firms to move from informal to formal activity without imposing burdens that discourage them from growing.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
2557 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (Education and vocational training should also be connected more directly to actual labour-market demand. The World Bank argues that improving productivity across agriculture, household enterprises, and modern wage sectors is essential to addressing Africa's youth employment challenge.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
2558 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (Recent African Development Bank work similarly emphasises skills, private investment, entrepreneurship and access to finance as important parts of turning Africa's young population into an economic asset.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
2559 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (The policy goal should not be to push every young person toward formal employment. It should be to create an economy in which agriculture, small businesses, self-employment and formal wage jobs can all become more productive, secure and capable of generating rising incomes.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
2560 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1039 0 R
/T (Make elections more credible, not simply more frequent)
/E (Make elections more credible, not simply more frequent)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
2561 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
2562 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (The priority should therefore move beyond simply holding elections. Governments and electoral management bodies should ensure that electoral commissions have genuine operational independence, that voter registration is accessible and transparent, that political parties compete on a level playing field and that electoral disputes can be resolved quickly by independent courts.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
2563 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (African countries should also strengthen campaign-finance transparency, protect election observers and journalists, and publish election results in accessible and verifiable formats. Regional institutions should make democratic commitments enforceable. International IDEA has also called for stronger regional mechanisms, constitutional safeguards and accessible electoral justice systems.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
2564 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1039 0 R
/T (Treat digital access as both an economic and political freedom)
/E (Treat digital access as both an economic and political freedom)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
2565 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (Digital connectivity has become central to economic participation, education, communication and civic life. F\(in\)AR finds that mobile technology is already contributing substantially to Africa's economy, yet affordability remains a major barrier and internet shutdowns continue to restrict access. The report records 30 shutdowns across 15 African countries.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
2566 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (Governments should commit to keeping the internet open, affordable and reliable, including during elections and periods of political unrest. Internet shutdowns should not be used as a routine tool for managing protests or political activity.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
2567 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (Cybercrime laws should target genuine criminal conduct without becoming broad instruments for suppressing criticism or journalism. Governments should also strengthen privacy protections and establish clear rules governing state surveillance and emerging technologies.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
2568 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (At the economic level, governments should reduce the cost of devices and data, expand broadband infrastructure and encourage competition among telecommunications providers. Connecting people is not enough; citizens must also be able to afford meaningful use of the internet.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
2569 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1039 0 R
/T (Protect civic space, independent media and peaceful protest)
/E (Protect civic space, independent media and peaceful protest)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
2570 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (Freedom of expression, association and assembly are essential for accountable governance. Governments should review laws affecting journalists, civil society organisations and public demonstrations to ensure that restrictions are necessary, proportionate and consistent with democratic principles. )
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
2571 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (Security agencies should receive clear guidance on managing peaceful protests, while allegations of excessive force should be investigated independently. Expanding civic space allows citizens to participate constructively in public life and provides governments with valuable feedback on policy performance.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [25]
>>
endobj
2572 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (06           F\(in\)AR 2026          36)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [26]
>>
endobj
2573 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1039 0 R
/E (Policy Recommendation)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
