2576 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 1040 0 R
/Pg 46 0 R
/A [5498 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
2577 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (Protect property rights and make it easier to do business)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2578 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (Secure property rights and predictable business regulations are critical for investment and economic growth. Governments should simplify business registration processes, reduce unnecessary licensing requirements and improve land registration systems. )
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2579 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (Digital public services can help reduce administrative burdens and increase transparency. Stronger protection of property rights gives households and businesses greater confidence to invest, while a more competitive business environment can encourage innovation, entrepreneurship and job creation.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2580 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1040 0 R
/T (Make AfCFTA work through practical reforms)
/E (Make AfCFTA work through practical reforms)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2581 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (The benefits of continental integration will depend on implementation rather than agreements alone. Governments should reduce customs delays, simplify border procedures and remove non-tariff barriers that increase the cost of trade. Investment in transport corridors, logistics infrastructure and digital trade systems can help businesses access larger markets. Regional economic communities should also promote easier movement of workers, mutual recognition of qualifications and interoperable payment systems. These reforms would help translate AfCFTA into tangible economic opportunities for citizens and businesses.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2582 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1040 0 R
/T (Give young people a meaningful stake in government)
/E (Give young people a meaningful stake in government)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
2583 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (Africa's youth are increasingly using protests, social media, community organisations and environmental campaigns to influence public policy. F\(in\)AR shows that this participation is expanding even as civic space remains restricted.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
2584 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (Governments should create formal channels through which young people can influence policy beyond elections. Youth councils, citizen consultations, participatory budgeting, digital consultation platforms and structured dialogue between governments and civil society can provide alternatives to street mobilisation when grievances emerge.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
2585 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (International IDEA's recent work also emphasises meaningful youth participation, civic education and political literacy, particularly for young and first-time voters. Young people should not be viewed only as beneficiaries of policy. They should be treated as participants in designing it.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
2586 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1040 0 R
/T (Make prosperity about services, security and opportunity)
/E (Make prosperity about services, security and opportunity)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
2587 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (F\(in\)AR's prosperity findings provide one of the report's clearest warnings. Improvements in some areas were outweighed by setbacks in employment, equality, healthcare access, and peace and stability. The report's prosperity results show that economic growth alone does not guarantee better living conditions.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
2588 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (Governments should therefore measure development by outcomes that citizens experience directly: access to healthcare, quality education, reliable electricity and transport, employment opportunities, personal security and the ability to improve one's economic position. Public investment should prioritise infrastructure and essential services that increase productivity while also reaching underserved communities.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
2589 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (Inequality should be treated as an economic policy issue as well as a social one. The World Bank argues that widening access to jobs and public services is one of Africa's strongest opportunities for reducing poverty and expanding growth. Growth becomes more meaningful when more people can participate in it.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
2590 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1040 0 R
/T (Put peace and security at the centre of development policy)
/E (Put peace and security at the centre of development policy)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
2591 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (The FinAR data shows a strong connection between instability and weak prosperity. Conflict disrupts businesses, displaces communities and makes it harder for governments to provide basic services. The Institute for Economics and Peace identifies Sub-Saharan Africa as one of the world's least peaceful regions, with eight of the 20 least peaceful countries globally. Security policy should therefore be linked to economic and institutional development rather than treated as a separate policy area.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
2592 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (Governments should strengthen civilian institutions, improve local conflict-prevention mechanisms and address the economic pressures that contribute to instability. In conflict-affected countries, restoring basic security and functioning public institutions should come before expecting major improvements in investment, employment or public services. Regional organisations should strengthen early-warning systems and coordinate responses to political crises before instability becomes entrenched.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
2593 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1040 0 R
/T (A coordinated African policy agenda)
/E (A coordinated African policy agenda)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
2594 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (The central lesson of F\(in\)AR 2026 is that Africa does not need to choose between freedom and development. The evidence instead suggests that political rights, economic opportunity and prosperity should be treated as mutually reinforcing parts of development. For stronger-performing countries, the priority is to widen participation in existing gains through better jobs, infrastructure, private-sector growth and more inclusive public services. For countries facing democratic pressure, the priority is to rebuild institutional trust, protect civic freedoms and strengthen electoral and judicial systems. For fragile and conflict-affected states, restoring security, basic institutions and essential services must come first.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
2595 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (At the continental level, five priorities should guide the next phase of reform:)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
2596 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 5499 0 R
/E (First, build capable and accountable institutions. Governments must strengthen rule of law, judicial independence, electoral integrity, and public accountability.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
2597 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 5500 0 R
/E (Second, expand economic opportunity. Countries should make it easier to start businesses, protect property, access finance, trade, and create productive employment.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
2598 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 5501 0 R
/E (Third, invest in people. Education, healthcare, infrastructure and skills development must be treated as foundations of prosperity rather than secondary outcomes of economic growth.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
2599 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 5502 0 R
/E (Fourth, keep Africa connected. Regional mobility, AfCFTA implementation, digital infrastructure, and open internet access should become central elements of economic and civic policy.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
2600 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 5503 0 R
/E (Fifth, protect participation. Citizens must have meaningful opportunities to vote, organise, protest, access information, and influence public decisions without fear of repression.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
2601 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1040 0 R
/E (The greatest opportunity presented by F\(in\)AR 2026 is therefore not simply identifying which countries rank highest or lowest. It is identifying what stronger-performing countries are doing well, where weaker countries face recurring barriers, and how those lessons can be adapted across different African contexts. African countries must create the confidence for investment, as economic opportunity will create productive livelihoods and public services will convert economic gains into better living conditions. Enhanced Security must then protect these gains. And citizens must have the freedom to participate in the systems that govern them.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [25]
>>
endobj
2602 0 obj
<<
